From f2677995a57f394af2e138a8701f5f4986025a15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:25:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [BUGFIX] Resources: bound the parsed tree (DoS on
 self-referencing and overlapping directories)

Since "Removed limit for resources to be parsed" the number of entries per
directory is unbounded, and only the depth (MAX_DEPTH 5) limits the tree.
A directory whose entries point back at itself (or at the top directory), or a
chain of distinct directories whose N entries all point at the next one, makes
the parsed tree grow as N^MAX_DEPTH. With N=100 the parser does not return and
memory grows until the process is killed (hasherezade/pe-bear#77).

Two bounds, neither of which limits a well-formed resource tree:

1. Every directory is expanded at most once per tree (set of raw offsets held
   by the top directory). This removes cycles and shared children.
2. The tree has a budget of getRawSize() / sizeof(IMAGE_RESOURCE_DIRECTORY_ENTRY)
   entries. In a well-formed tree every entry occupies its own bytes, so it cannot
   be exceeded. It is needed because directories of a malformed file may overlap
   (a directory header placed over the entries of another directory), so that
   distinct offsets still share the same bytes as entries: (1) alone does not stop that.

Both are reset by the top directory's wrap(), which is the only place
a resource tree is rewrapped.

Known behaviour change: a directory referenced by more than one entry is
expanded only under the first one.

Add tests/gen_rsrc_poc.py, which generates synthetic PE32 files for the three
shapes (selfloop, chain, overlap). Command: `rsl` in bearcommander.
- base (c18e939):          selfloop, chain: no result within 20 s
- visited-set only:        overlap (20000 entries): no result within 30 s
- this commit:             all three finish in < 0.1 s
Output of `rsl` and `rstrings` on the 25 PE files of bearparser_tests is identical
before and after (few of them have resources, so this is weak evidence for
shared-directory cases).

